# No third-person Paragon narration

Do **not** write prose that treats Paragon as a third-person actor (for example "Paragon labels…", "Paragon has…", "Paragon enables…", "Paragon enforces…", "At Paragon, …").

## Preferred voice

Use **passive** (or direct second-person instructions) so the subject is the capability, setting, or reader:

| Avoid | Prefer |
| :--- | :--- |
| Paragon labels the approved entries in two groups | Approved entries fall into two practical groups |
| Paragon has two source control providers enabled | Two source control providers are enabled for the team |
| Paragon enables GitLab and Azure DevOps at team level | GitLab and Azure DevOps are enabled at team level |
| Paragon enforces three Required team rules | Three Required team rules are enforced in the **Admin Panel** |
| At Paragon, use only admin-approved triggers | Use only admin-approved triggers |

## Allowed uses of "Paragon"

Keep "Paragon" when it is a **name or scope label**, not an actor:

- Proper nouns and account wording: Paragon company account, Paragon GitLab tenant, `paragon-cc`
- Possessive naming of artefacts: Paragon's Cursor organization, Paragon's Enterprise subscription
- Policy titles and links: AI02, "use of Cursor at Paragon is governed by…"
- Audience labels: Paragon users (when distinguishing from upstream Cursor docs)

## Out of scope

Do not rewrite formal policy text in `AI02-Business-Use-of-Artificial-Intelligence.md` solely for this rule. Historical changelog rows need not be backfilled.
